{:schema 1, :start-person "17592186053137", :analyses [{:clause-probes [{:clause [?person "knows" ?f], :probe-pattern ["17592186053137" "knows" nil], :rows 882, :probe-ms 3.014792} {:clause [?msg "hasCreator" ?f], :probe-pattern [nil "hasCreator" nil], :rows 7355, :probe-ms 45.524834} {:clause [?msg "message/creationDate" ?date], :probe-pattern [nil "message/creationDate" nil], :rows 7355, :probe-ms 138.257875}], :end-to-end-ms 403.582709, :plan-ms 78.930667, :probe-total-ms 186.797501, :chosen-order [{:step 0, :clause [?person "knows" ?f], :estimated-rows 882, :estimate-source :visible-scan} {:step 1, :clause [?msg "hasCreator" ?f], :estimated-rows 7355, :estimate-source :visible-scan} {:step 2, :clause [?msg "message/creationDate" ?date], :estimated-rows 7355, :estimate-source :visible-scan}], :end-to-end-rows 910, :execute-rows 910, :query :ic09-one-hop, :execute-ms 818.853709} {:clause-probes [{:clause [?person "knows" ?f1], :probe-pattern ["17592186053137" "knows" nil], :rows 882, :probe-ms 14.540625} {:clause [?f1 "knows" ?f2], :probe-pattern [nil "knows" nil], :rows 361246, :probe-ms 3859.710459} {:clause [?msg "hasCreator" ?f2], :probe-pattern [nil "hasCreator" nil], :rows 7355, :probe-ms 68.574833} {:clause [?msg "message/creationDate" ?date], :probe-pattern [nil "message/creationDate" nil], :rows 7355, :probe-ms 100.375125}], :end-to-end-ms 45307.619042, :plan-ms 3358.71575, :probe-total-ms 4043.201042, :chosen-order [{:step 0, :clause [?person "knows" ?f1], :estimated-rows 882, :estimate-source :visible-scan} {:step 1, :clause [?f1 "knows" ?f2], :estimated-rows 361246, :estimate-source :visible-scan} {:step 2, :clause [?msg "hasCreator" ?f2], :estimated-rows 7355, :estimate-source :visible-scan} {:step 3, :clause [?msg "message/creationDate" ?date], :estimated-rows 7355, :estimate-source :visible-scan}], :end-to-end-rows 6733, :execute-rows 6733, :query :ic09-two-hop, :execute-ms 35641.37175}], :adr "ADR-2608021000 §6-1 -- print the plan before touching anything", :caveats ["Single start person, single run per measurement -- this is a diagnosis, not a benchmark. The receipt with percentiles is 2026-08-01-ldbc-snb-interactive.edn." "end-to-end-ms goes through kotobase-peer.core/query, which plans AND executes, so it should be roughly probe-total + plan overhead + execute." "Host load is recorded by whoever runs this; probe and execute are measured back to back, so their RATIO is meaningful even when the machine is busy."], :receipt/type :ic09-diagnosis, :reading "Compare probe-total-ms against execute-ms per query. The probes are what datalog-query-plan pays to ESTIMATE cardinality, by running each clause; execute-ms is the join itself. If the probes dominate, the cost is planning, not joining, and the fix is to stop estimating by full materialisation -- not to reorder anything.", :implementation "kotobase-peer.ic09-diag", :dataset {:subset-posts 3000, :quads 409877, :knows-quads 361246, :messages 7355}
 :findings
 ["The planner is NOT the problem. It picked the right first clause (the one with :in bound, 882 rows) and the greedy connected order is forced from there. Its cardinality probes cost 4.0 s of the 45.3 s -- real, but 9%."
  "One probe is worth fixing on its own: [?f1 \"knows\" ?f2] has both ends unbound, so datalog-query-plan estimates it by RUNNING it -- 361,246 rows materialized into a set, 3.9 s, on every single query, purely to learn a number. Materialized statistics (:query-statistics) already exist as an input and would skip this entirely."
  "The dominant cost is the join: 35.6 s of 45.3 s. arrangement.datalog/join-clause issued ONE scan per BINDING. The two-hop step carries ~31,750 bindings while those bindings substitute down to at most a few thousand distinct patterns, so most of those scans asked the same question again."
  "The one-hop control confirms the shape rather than the machine: same data, same process, 187 ms of probes and 819 ms of join. The blow-up is specific to the step whose binding count explodes."]
 :fix-landed "arrangement.datalog/join-clause now groups bindings by substituted pattern and issues one scan per DISTINCT pattern. Semantics-preserving: bindings sharing a pattern are unified against the same rows individually."
 :not-fixed
 ["Scan-per-distinct-pattern is still index-nested-loop. When a clause's whole relation is small (hasCreator is 7,355 rows here) a single scan plus a hash join beats thousands of seeks, and the planner ALREADY computes the number needed to choose -- :estimated-rows per clause. Threading that hint into the executor is the next step, and it is the one that should close the gap to Neo4j's 246 ms."
  "Projection pushdown: IC09's :find is [?f2 ?msg ?date] but bindings carry ?f1 all the way through, so ~31,750 bindings exist where <=9,892 distinct ?f2 would do."]
 :reading-notes
 ["end-to-end-ms exceeds probe-total + plan + execute because it re-runs the whole plan-and-execute cycle through kotobase-peer.core/query after the parts were timed separately."]}
